    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ottoman Turkish was the Turkish of the empire’s court, poetry and administration, so full of Arabic and Persian that words from them made up as much as 88% of its vocabulary.</li><li>It was largely unintelligible to rural Turks, who spoke kaba Türkçe, “rough Turkish”, with far fewer loanwords, the basis of modern standard Turkish.</li><li>In 1928, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> replaced the Perso-Arabic script with an extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet and began replacing Arabic and Persian loanwords with Turkish ones.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The same speaker could use two languages for one thing: a scribe would write the Arabic word asel for honey in a document, but would ask for it by the Turkish word bal when buying it.</p></aside>
    </section>

